aeltra-distro-tools (1.19.0-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * package: aeltra-pack gives every file and directory of a package a
    default mode unless the package file states one (mode="..."), as
    dh_fixperms of debhelper does. Until now the mode was taken from
    the install folder, so it depended on the umask of the build and on
    the build system of the sources. The default is 0755 for a directory
    and for a file that somebody may execute, 0644 for any other file. A
    setuid, setgid or sticky bit is dropped, and what group and others
    could not read is opened to them: aeltra-pack warns about each such
    file, the build goes on. A recipe has to state these modes in its
    package files to keep them.
  * package: the rules of dh_fixperms for places and names apply too.
    Not executable: documentation (but examples), manual pages, headers,
    desktop files, themes, Perl modules, and libraries (*.so, *.so.*,
    *.a, *.la), scripts for the web and images by their names.
    Executable: what is in bin, sbin, games, libexec and /etc/init.d.
    Files in /etc/sudoers.d are 0440. A shared library that has to stay
    executable needs its mode in the package file, the libc.so of musl
    for example, which is the dynamic loader too.
  * package: tests for the above (package/tests), run by tox like the
    ones of the other parts.

 -- Tobias Koch <tobias.koch@gmail.com>  Mon, 23 Nov 2026 23:38:52 +0300

aeltra-distro-tools (1.18.1-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * image-gen: the minimal and build-essential specs no longer name
    pam-config, which is gone. busybox now requires libpam-runtime, and
    that brings the PAM modules and pam-utils, whose pam-auth-update
    writes /etc/pam.d/common-*. Needs a busybox package with that
    dependency, with an older one the images have no PAM modules.

 -- Tobias Koch <tobias.koch@gmail.com>  Sat, 21 Nov 2026 23:32:17 +0300

aeltra-distro-tools (1.18.0-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * package: bh_python_install no longer runs "setup.py install", which
    is gone with distutils in Python 3.12 and current setuptools. It
    builds a wheel by calling the build backend named in pyproject.toml
    and installs it with the installer module, into
    lib/python3/dist-packages. The options --build, --install and --root
    work as before, --py3 is accepted and ignored, --py2 is gone.
    Arguments for setup.py after "--" are refused. A recipe that uses
    the helper has to require python3-installer and the build backend of
    its project (python3-setuptools or python3-flit-core).

 -- Tobias Koch <tobias.koch@gmail.com>  Fri, 20 Nov 2026 23:28:59 +0300

aeltra-distro-tools (1.17.0-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * package: a maintainer or trigger script in a recipe takes the
    attribute interpreter="sh" (the default) or interpreter="busybox".
    The second is for scripts that have to work without the link
    /bin/sh, which are those of busybox; their first line is
    "#!/bin/busybox sh", or "#!/tools/bin/busybox sh" in a tools
    package. Any other value is refused. Needs aept 0.11.0 or later on
    the system the package is installed on, older versions run every
    script with /bin/sh.
  * package: "set -e" is a line of its own in the scripts aeltra-pack
    writes, no longer an argument in the first line, where the kernel
    passes only one. With aept 0.11.0 it is in effect for the first
    time: a script now stops at the first failing command.
  * image-gen: the minimal and build-essential specs name busybox-binsh,
    busybox-suid and busybox-ssl-client. The busybox-*-symlinks packages
    are gone, a trigger of the busybox package creates the links.

 -- Tobias Koch <tobias.koch@gmail.com>  Fri, 20 Nov 2026 09:45:08 +0300

aeltra-distro-tools (1.16.1-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * package: bh_gcc_config_for_machine configures GCC with --disable-cet
    for x86_64 musl targets. musl does not support CET, the markers in
    GCC's runtime libraries would be dead weight there. glibc targets
    keep --enable-cet.

 -- Tobias Koch <tobias.koch@gmail.com>  Wed, 18 Nov 2026 22:41:46 +0300

aeltra-distro-tools (1.16.0-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * distro-info: AELTRA_CONFIG_URL names another location to fetch
    releases.json and mirrors.json from than archive.aeltra.eu, for
    example a local archive during a bootstrap. Its lists are cached in
    ~/.aeltra/config/<tag>, with <tag> being the first 8 hex digits of
    the SHA-256 of the URL, so the regular lists are left alone and
    switching between locations needs no cleanup. URLs are normalised
    before they are compared and hashed, and anything but a plain http
    or https URL is refused.
  * misc: the downloader's cache tag includes the URL. Two files from a
    server that sends no ETag and the same modification time got the
    same tag, and the second download replaced the cached copy of the
    first. Existing tags change with this, so cached files are fetched
    once more.
  * Only the six supported architectures are known: aarch64, riscv64
    and x86_64 (primary), loongarch64, powerpc64le and s390x
    (secondary). loongarch64 is new, the 32 bit and mips architectures
    are gone.
  * misc: stack clash protection is only requested on aarch64 and
    x86_64, and control flow protection on x86_64, as dpkg does.
  * package: bh_gcc_config_for_machine follows the GCC configuration of
    Debian forky and needs the GCC from there: PIE by default on all
    architectures, CET on x86_64, the RVA20U64 baseline on riscv64, no
    excess float precision on s390x. On powerpc64le, glibc targets get
    an IEEE 128 bit long double, musl targets keep the 64 bit one. The
    function expects a target triplet now, to tell the two apart, and
    fails on anything else.

 -- Tobias Koch <tobias.koch@gmail.com>  Tue, 17 Nov 2026 18:38:57 +0300

aeltra-distro-tools (1.15.0-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * misc: the downloader can send credentials. Downloader takes an
    AuthFile, read from a file in apt's auth.conf format as aept reads
    it, and sends the login and password of a matching entry as Basic
    auth on GET and HEAD. Only https URLs match, by host and path
    prefix, and the header isn't passed on to redirects. Without an
    AuthFile nothing changes: which downloads carry credentials is up
    to each tool.
  * ffi-libarchive: ArchiveFileReader reads from a file descriptor as
    well, and from the current entry of another reader: an archive
    inside an archive is decompressed as it is read, without being
    written out anywhere.
  * ffi-libarchive: unpack_to_disk() could write outside its target
    directory, through ".." in a name or through a symlink the archive
    had just created, and it kept setuid, setgid and sticky bits.
    libarchive's disk writer does the writing now and refuses both,
    and the special bits are dropped. Directories, regular files and
    symlinks are unpacked as before, with their exact modes; anything
    else is still refused. Absolute names end up below the target
    directory, and entries that map to the directory itself are
    skipped.
  * ffi-libarchive: names that are missing, aren't UTF-8 or can't be
    converted to the locale's charset no longer raise AttributeError or
    UnicodeDecodeError, or fail the whole archive under LC_ALL=C. A
    missing name is None, and bytes that aren't UTF-8 are kept with
    surrogateescape, so that they are unpacked as they were.
  * ffi-libarchive: closing a writer reports errors, such as a full
    disk, instead of silently leaving an incomplete archive behind, and
    so does an entry that gets more or less data than its size, e.g. a
    file that changed while it was being packed.
  * ffi-libarchive: no more crashes from using an entry after the loop
    that read it has moved on, or from a failed allocation, and a
    signal arriving while an inner archive is being read is no longer
    swallowed. Times beyond 2038 and the 64-bit fields are passed with
    the types of libarchive's headers. uid and clone() work.
  * Tests: misc and ffi-libarchive have pytest suites now. tox runs
    them as py3, and flake8 as lint.

 -- Tobias Koch <tobias.koch@gmail.com>  Fri, 13 Nov 2026 00:24:55 +0300

